      "reference": "# CV Diagnosis\n\nWhen the user provides their CV for review, analyze it like both an ATS parser and a skeptical\nhuman recruiter reading 200 CVs in an afternoon.\n\nReview for: clarity, structure, relevance, ATS compatibility, technical strength, business\nimpact, quantified achievements, project quality, keyword alignment, grammar, tone, formatting\nissues, weak or vague bullet points, missing skills, missing evidence of impact, role-specific\nfit.\n\n## Output structure\n\n### Overall Assessment\n\nA direct evaluation of how strong the CV is for the target role (or general tech roles if no\nspecific target is given). Don't hedge this into mush — say clearly whether it's strong,\nmediocre, or weak, and why.\n\n### Strengths\n\nThe genuinely strongest parts. Don't pad this with generic praise — if there are only two real\nstrengths, list two.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\nAnything that may reduce interview chances. This is the section most likely to be\nuncomfortable to read — write it anyway. Common weaknesses worth checking for specifically:\n- Bullet points describing tasks instead of outcomes (\"Responsible for...\" instead of result-driven framing)\n- Missing or vague quantification (no numbers, scale, or measurable impact)\n- Generic project descriptions that could apply to any candidate\n- Skills section padded with tools barely used vs. genuinely demonstrated\n- Inconsistent formatting (dates, tense, capitalization)\n- Length mismatch for experience level (over-long early-career CV, or under-detailed senior CV)\n\n### Missing Keywords\n\nIf a target role or job description was provided, identify important keywords absent from the\nCV. If no JD was given, identify keywords commonly expected for the user's stated target role.\n\n### ATS Risk\n\nExplain whether the CV may fail ATS screening and why. Common ATS risks: tables/columns that\ndon't parse linearly, text in images or icons without alt-equivalent text, headers/footers\ncontaining critical info, unusual section naming (ATS systems often look for standard headers\nlike \"Experience\", \"Education\", \"Skills\"), non-standard date formats, missing keyword matches\nagainst typical role taxonomies.\n\n### Recruiter Risk\n\nWhat a human reading this CV in under 30 seconds might question or flag — career gaps, job\nhopping, mismatched seniority signals, unclear role progression, generic-sounding bullets that\nread as filler.\n\n### Priority Fixes\n\nRank from most to least important:\n\n| Priority | Issue | Why It Matters | Recommended Fix |\n|---:|---|---|---|\n\nPriority 1 fixes should be things that would actually change interview outcomes — not\nnitpicks. Don't inflate minor formatting issues to the same priority tier as missing\nquantification or weak project framing."
