      "reference": "# Cover Letters & Outreach Messages\n\n## Cover Letters\n\nMake every cover letter:\n\n- Specific to the company (not a template with the company name swapped in)\n- Specific to the role\n- Professional but natural-sounding\n- Short enough to actually be read\n- Focused on value the user brings, not just enthusiasm\n- Connected to the user's real projects, skills, and background\n\n### Structure\n\n1. **Opening** — the role being applied for and why.\n2. **Fit** — relevant experience, technical skills, or projects, tied directly to what the\n   role needs.\n3. **Company connection** — why this company or role specifically interests the user (requires\n   genuine company-specific detail, not generic \"I admire your mission\" filler).\n4. **Closing** — confident and polite call to action.\n\n### Avoid\n\nGeneric filler like:\n> I am passionate about technology.\n\n### Use instead\n\nConcrete, specific sentences like:\n> My experience building machine learning pipelines, analyzing structured datasets, and\n> communicating technical findings aligns closely with the role's focus on data-driven\n> product improvement.\n\nDefault length: 250–400 words unless the user asks for a longer version.\n\n---\n\n## LinkedIn & Email Outreach Messages\n\nFor contacting recruiters, hiring managers, professors, founders, or employees:\n\n- Polite, clear, specific\n- Not desperate\n- Easy to reply to\n- Under 150 words where possible\n\n### Structure for networking messages\n\n1. Who the user is\n2. Why they're reaching out\n3. Relevant fit\n4. A simple, low-effort request\n\n### Example template\n\n> Hi [Name], I'm currently exploring [role type] opportunities in [field]. I noticed your\n> work at [company] focuses on [specific area], which aligns with my background in\n> [skill/project]. I'd be grateful for any advice on applying or understanding what the team\n> looks for in candidates. Thank you.\n\nCustomize the bracketed sections with real specifics from the user's background and the\nrecipient's actual work — a message that could be sent to anyone reads as low-effort and gets\nignored. If the user hasn't given enough detail about the recipient to personalize this,\nask rather than filling in something generic."
