      "reference": "# Interview Preparation\n\nCreate interview questions based on the specific role and job description provided — generic\nquestion banks are less useful than ones grounded in what this particular role/JD emphasizes.\n\n## HR / Behavioral Questions\n\nStandard set to prepare for regardless of role:\n- Tell me about yourself.\n- Why this role?\n- Why this company?\n- What are your strengths?\n- What are your weaknesses?\n- Tell me about a challenge you faced.\n- Tell me about a time you worked in a team.\n- Why should we hire you?\n\n## Technical Questions by Role\n\n### Data Analyst\nSQL, Excel, Power BI, Tableau, statistics, business case analysis, data cleaning, dashboard\ndesign, KPI interpretation.\n\n### Data Scientist\nMachine learning, model evaluation, feature engineering, Python, statistics, experimentation,\nregression, classification, overfitting, cross-validation, model explainability.\n\n### Data Engineer\nSQL, ETL, data pipelines, cloud platforms, data modeling, data warehousing, batch processing,\nworkflow orchestration, data quality.\n\n### Software Engineer\nBackend development, APIs, databases, system design, testing, Git, deployment, authentication,\nperformance, debugging.\n\n### AI Engineer\nLLMs, RAG, prompt engineering, vector databases, embeddings, evaluation, model APIs, AI\nagents, guardrails, cost optimization, latency optimization.\n\nWhen generating technical questions for a specific JD, prioritize topics the JD actually\nemphasizes over this generic list — if a Data Scientist JD heavily emphasizes A/B testing and\nexperimentation, weight questions there rather than spreading evenly across the whole category.\n\n## STAR Answers\n\nWhen the user asks for interview answers, use the STAR format:\n\n- **Situation** — brief context\n- **Task** — what needed to be done\n- **Action** — what the user specifically did (not \"we\")\n- **Result** — outcome, quantified where possible\n\nKeep answers natural and specific, grounded in the user's real projects and experience —\nnever invented scenarios. If the user's real experience doesn't cleanly map to a STAR\nstructure for a given question, say so and help them find the closest genuine example rather\nthan fabricating one."
